# {{TITLE_TEXT}}

This is a standalone mathematics-notes project built with LuaLaTeX. Its source,
style, editor configuration, and build commands all live in this directory.

## Local setup

Install a current TeX Live distribution that includes LuaLaTeX, `latexmk`, the
Chinese-language collection, and the common/extra LaTeX package collections.
Verify that the three required commands and core files resolve:

```sh
command -v lualatex latexmk kpsewhich
kpsewhich ctexbook.cls
kpsewhich qlmathnotes.sty
```

In native PowerShell, replace the first line with
`Get-Command lualatex,latexmk,kpsewhich`.

`qlmathnotes.sty` should resolve to the copy in this project. These checks are
read-only. Install VS Code plus the recommended `james-yu.latex-workshop` and
`yfzhao.ultra-math-preview` extensions for build-on-save, PDF viewing, SyncTeX,
and cursor-local formula previews.

## Work locally

```sh
latexmk -lualatex -synctex=1 -interaction=nonstopmode -halt-on-error \
  -file-line-error -outdir=build/latex main.tex
latexmk -C -outdir=build/latex main.tex
```

These commands and the included VS Code tasks work in macOS, Linux, WSL, and
native Windows terminals. On POSIX systems, `make build`, `make clean`, and
`make edit` are optional convenience wrappers; GNU Make and Python are not
project requirements.

The only document output is `build/latex/main.pdf`; `build/` is ignored by Git.
Compilation errors and warnings remain visible in both the terminal and LaTeX
Workshop. `main.tex`, `chapters/*.tex`, `qlmathnotes.sty`, and `reference.bib`
are the editable project authority.

## Authoring helpers

- Statements: `definition`, `theorem`, `lemma`, `proposition`, `corollary`,
  `example`, `problem`, `exercise`, `remark`, `note`, `proof`, and `solution`.
- Listings: `python`, `cpp`, `terminal`/`cmd`, and `txt`; inline Python and C++
  use `\pythoninline{...}` and `\cppinline{...}`.
- Images: put files in `assets/`, then use the standard `figure`,
  `\includegraphics`, `\caption`, and `\label` commands.
- Semantic text markers: `\kn{concept}` and `\knref{concept}` remain ordinary
  printable LaTeX commands.

To enable the bibliography, uncomment the BibLaTeX lines in `main.tex`, cite
entries from `reference.bib`, and uncomment `\printbibliography`.
